/-!
# Carries held in flags against materialised carries, on the P-256 template multiply

The value translation validation `OwnedLprogTVChecked.ownedCheckMC` compares a body whose
carries are materialised against one whose carries are held in the carry flag through the
canonical keys of its term table (`OwnedLprogTV.canonEqT`, `OwnedLprogTV.SG.impT`). This
module shows it on the template field multiply at the P-256 base prime, the program of
`FieldInstances.p256_mul_correct_bytes`.

## Main results

* `p256MulRef` is the reference body that the image check `renImageB` of the region back half
  hands to `ownedCheckMC` for that program (`p256MulRef_eq`). Its carries are materialised:
  each carry step is a three-source `ADCX:64 d, [a, b, c]` and its carry-out `ADCXHI:64`, with
  the width-1 zero `MOV^1 c, #0` at the start of a chain.
* `p256MulFlags` is the same computation with its carry chains in the carry flag, a test input
  for the checker written once from `p256MulRef`: each chain starts with the two-source
  `ADD:64 d, [a, b]` and continues with the two-source `ADCX:64 d, [a, b]` reading the carry
  flag, and the carry-out bindings `ADCXHI:64` are gone. The last chain, whose carry-out is the
  condition of the four `CMOV:64` of the final reduction, keeps its carries in registers: the
  flag machine has no instruction that moves the carry flag into a register.
* `p256Mul_flags_accepted`: `ownedCheckMC` accepts the pair; `p256Mul_flags_leak`: so does the
  traced check `ownedCheckML`, which also compares the leakage events, so the flag form adds no
  event; `p256Mul_flags_sound`: the simulation of `ownedCheckMC_sound` at the pair.
* `twoLimbValue`, `twoLimbFlags`, `twoLimb_refused`: a two-limb addition of start registers in
  the two forms, which the checker refuses. The flag form `ADC:64` reads its sources at width
  `64`, and nothing in the value form fixes the width of a start register, so the refusal is
  required by the semantics: the value form also runs on words of other widths.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

